omp-web (0.3.10) jammy; urgency=medium

  * debian/postinst: fix `ensure_env()` to also copy the package's
    `patches/` directory (if it ships one) into `env_dir` alongside
    `package.json`/`bun.lock`, before running `bun install
    --frozen-lockfile --production` there. `bun.lock`'s
    `patchedDependencies` entries reference patch files by a path relative
    to the install cwd (e.g.
    `patches/@oh-my-pi%2Fpi-coding-agent@17.3.0.patch`); since `ensure_env`
    (new in 0.3.9) never copied `patches/` into the freshly materialized
    env dir, `bun install --frozen-lockfile` failed with "Couldn't find
    patch file" on every host's very first run of `ensure_env` — i.e.
    every currently-deployed host's first upgrade to 0.3.9, leaving dpkg
    half-configured. Found via a throwaway-LXC fresh-install verification
    pass before any production host upgraded.
  * tests/run-postinst-fixture-tests.sh: the fake `bun install` now
    validates that any patch file referenced by `bun.lock` exists relative
    to the install cwd (as real `bun install` does), and a new fixture
    case seeds a `patches/` directory with a marker file referenced from a
    `patchedDependencies`-shaped `bun.lock` entry, asserting the marker
    file lands inside the materialized `env_dir` and `bun install`
    succeeds.

 -- JoySort Release Bot <release@joysort.cc>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 13:08:59 +0000

omp-web (0.3.9) jammy; urgency=medium

  * debian/rules, debian/postinst: stop shipping `node_modules` (2.2GB) and
    `.next/cache` (590MB, pure build cache `next start` never reads) inside
    the .deb — excluded from `override_dh_auto_install`'s rsync. Instead
    `postinst`'s new `ensure_env()` (called from `sync_app()`, right after
    the tar-overlay and before the possible service restart) materializes
    `node_modules` on the target host into a `bun.lock`-hash-keyed shared
    directory (`~/omp/envs/<sha256-prefix>`, `bun install
    --frozen-lockfile --production` run as the target user) and symlinks
    `~/omp/ompweb/node_modules` into it. An upgrade whose `bun.lock` is
    unchanged from the currently installed release reuses the existing env
    dir and skips the install entirely; a release with a different
    `bun.lock` gets its own env dir without disturbing the previous one. A
    pre-existing real (non-symlink) `node_modules` — the case for every
    currently-deployed host, which had it tar-overlaid directly before
    this change — is removed first, since `ln -sfn` targeting an existing
    real directory nests the new symlink inside it rather than replacing
    it. Also reclaims any stale `.next/cache` left on disk by a
    pre-existing host's earlier full-tree overlay, since the tar-overlay
    never deletes (closes #43).
  * tests/run-postinst-fixture-tests.sh: extend the fixture harness with a
    fake `bun` that logs invocations and materializes a `node_modules`
    marker on `install`, plus five new cases covering `ensure_env`'s
    first-materialization, unchanged-lockfile reuse, changed-lockfile
    reinstall/retarget, the stale-`.next/cache` cleanup, and a legacy real
    (non-symlink) `node_modules` being replaced by the correct symlink on
    upgrade.

